          "heading": "Impulse and step responses",
          "content": "# Unit 2, Lesson 1: Impulse and step responses\n\n**Unit theme:** Linear time-invariant systems in the time domain  \n**Level:** Undergraduate engineering (idealized models)  \n**Learning objective:** In LTI models, an impulse response h characterizes the zero-state input-output map; the step response integrates or sums the impulse response.\n\n## 1. Concept and mathematical language\n\nIn LTI models, an impulse response h characterizes the zero-state input-output map; the step response integrates or sums the impulse response. The role of this principle in **linear time-invariant systems in the time domain** is to connect the expression on paper with an observable prediction. Before manipulating symbols, define the independent variable, the range of allowed inputs, the output quantity, and the reference condition. In particular, identify whether the argument concerns a continuous-time signal. The same algebraic shape may describe completely different physical situations when time is measured in seconds in one case and by integer samples in another. A meaningful graph must identify its axes and units, the origin of the record, and whether the plotted values are exact or illustrative.\n\n## 2. A quantitative example\n\n**Checkable numerical or symbolic example:** For h(t)=5e^(\u22125t)u(t), step response is (1\u2212e^(\u22125t))u(t), reaching 63.2% at 0.2 s. Explain what the chosen values mean, list any units, and reproduce the result without replacing the governing mathematical relation with a memorized rule. A numerical example answers a narrowly framed question; it does not by itself certify an entire signal-processing chain. Describe the source or test signal, observation interval, reference time, and the output that would be compared with the calculated answer. If the expression uses a delta or step, say whether it represents an ideal mathematical distribution or a realizable finite-width experiment.\n\n## 3. Derivation strategy and model boundaries\n\nStart from the smallest version of the problem that keeps its essential structure. Represent a pulse with known support; annotate the independent coordinate and the nonzero support or spectral components. Derive the relevant expression step by step, naming the rule used to move from one line to the next. When integrating or summing, show the limits; when using a transform, write its kernel and normalization convention. If the quantity is complex, separate magnitude and phase before interpreting it as a real measurement. Avoid silently switching between angular frequency \u03c9 and cyclic frequency f.\n\nA **model boundary** is a statement about where the prediction can fail. For this lesson, pay particular attention to the following: A physical impulse has finite area and duration; a Dirac distribution is an ideal mathematical object. A useful report says whether the discrepancy originates in a violated hypothesis, finite measurement resolution, numerical rounding, a different Fourier or transform convention, or an unmodeled physical effect. Fitting a parameter after observing a failure does not retrospectively validate the original prediction. State any fitted parameters separately from independently measured ones.\n\n## 4. Engineering investigation\n\n**Task:** Compare a narrow calibrated pulse measurement against the predicted impulse area. Begin by writing a one-paragraph specification of the input, expected output, and permitted error. Choose an observable output rather than a qualitative claim such as \u201cthe plot looks correct.\u201d If the application is **RC transients and sliding-window smoothing**, explain what sensor or computation would provide the input and how the result could inform a decision. List relevant data units, an example operating range, and one adverse condition. Do not treat a synthetic input as a measurement of a real device.\n\nConnect the investigation to **convolution support and numerical quadrature**. Specify what a genuinely independent verification would require: a different derivation, a second calculation package, or a controlled instrument measurement with calibrated reference. When measurements are possible, identify bandwidth, resolution and uncertainty before interpreting a small residual as meaningful. When physical testing is unavailable, label all results \u201canalytical\u201d or \u201csynthetic simulation.\u201d A design choice should remain provisional until its assumptions have survived checks under conditions resembling the intended application.\n\n## 5. Interpretation and common mistakes\n\n**Pitfall to diagnose:** A physical impulse has finite area and duration; a Dirac distribution is an ideal mathematical object. If an equation is linear, verify linearity on two different input functions instead of one. A second frequent mistake is confusing a transformation's algebraic existence with its physical realizability. An ideal bandlimited spectrum, an impulse with zero duration, infinite observation time and perfect reconstruction are mathematical conveniences. Each may be useful, but laboratory claims must include finite time, bandwidth, quantization and uncertainty when relevant. This distinction becomes essential when a simulation is used to select acquisition hardware or establish safety margins.\n\n## 6. Guided exercises and assessment\n\n1. **Reconstruction:** Starting from the definitions, derive the relation used in the quantitative example. State every assumption, not only the final substitution. Reproduce a dimensional or normalization check.\n2. **Boundary test:** Recompute the lesson's core prediction for a zero input and a doubled input parameter where meaningful. Explain whether the expected change follows from linearity, dimensional reasoning, or a different property.\n3. **Counterexample:** Design a case in which **impulse and step responses** would be incorrectly applied. Name the violated assumption and the observable symptom.\n4. **Independent result:** Design a calculation or simulation that would check the mathematical expression without copying its exact implementation. Provide numerical values and expected units.\n5. **Application decision:** For **RC transients and sliding-window smoothing**, propose a measurement or controlled experiment that would distinguish the simplified model from one plausible alternative. Include a measurable acceptance criterion.\n\n### Self-check and marking guidance\n\nAn adequate answer identifies signal type and variables; states the relevant model equation; shows intermediate steps; reports units and reference convention; handles one limit or counterexample; and distinguishes analytic prediction from experimental verification. Work that quotes a final value without hypotheses is incomplete. A passing simulation alone does not establish the physical correctness of its underlying model. Assess whether the calculation would still be valid under a different sample rate, initial state or signal class, whichever is relevant here.\n\n**Editorial note:** These examples are for instruction. Their idealized component values and invented datasets are not design specifications for safety-critical, mains-connected, medical or certified systems.\n"
